WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.286
Windows Internet Name Service, or WINS, resolves NetBIOS names to IP addresses.

00:06.286 --> 00:12.148
This was the common method used to resolve hostnames to IP addresses before DNS came to the forefront.

00:12.148 --> 00:19.159
Nowadays, you only see WINS used basically on apps that were most likely built a long time ago and still use WINS.

00:19.159 --> 00:25.513
Most of the time now, DNS is the preferred method of resolving hostnames to IP addresses.

00:25.513 --> 00:31.463
We have established that DNS is the primary method of resolving host names to IP addresses.

00:31.463 --> 00:38.135
For the exam and for real-life situations, it is important to know the order in which hostnames are resolved.

00:38.135 --> 00:48.726
Here is that order:  Before looking at DNS, a host checks to see if the HOSTS file, which we will look at shortly, has a hostname to IP address mapping.

00:48.726 --> 01:03.330
HOSTS files are used when a DNS entry is not available or wanted for resolving hostnames to IP addresses. The DNS server indicated under one's IP address profile is looked at next.

01:03.330 --> 01:12.855
If there are multiple DNS servers listed, they will be tried, in order. If none of the local DNS server addresses resolve the name,

01:12.855 --> 01:20.685
the query is forwarded until a DNS server with the matching IP address is found and then the information is returned to the source.

